-- Shipping quote schema (docs/plans/cart-checkout.md §3 "Migration 0031", §4.5, session 7):
-- products.shipping_weight_grams, the eight locked-quote columns on orders, Komerce destination
-- columns on shipping_areas, customer_addresses.subdistrict and the shipping_rate_cache table.
-- Generated by drizzle-kit, then read; only this header and the lock_timeout line are hand-added.
-- No reference rows: the courier allowlist and the subsidy cap are code constants.
--
-- Backward compatibility with the previous release (session 6; CLAUDE.md expand/contract):
-- every new column is nullable or, for shipping_areas.last_synced_at, NOT NULL with a
-- non-volatile default, now() (no table rewrite on Postgres 11+). The previous release's INSERT/UPDATE statements
-- name only the columns they know, so the new ones stay NULL, and every new CHECK holds for that:
-- products_shipping_weight_grams_check and the orders quote checks all pass on NULL, and
-- orders_checkout_shipping_quote_check only binds checkout orders (payment_status set), which no
-- released code writes (placeOrder is session 9). If a database somehow held a checkout order
-- without a quote, that ADD CONSTRAINT fails and the whole migration rolls back untouched.
-- Nothing is dropped except two rules on shipping_areas, both loosened, never tightened: the
-- (province, city, district, postal_code) UNIQUE (two kelurahan can share all four; replaced by
-- UNIQUE komerce_destination_id) and NOT NULL on postal_code. The table is empty and no released
-- code reads or writes it. Rollback leaves the new columns and table in place, unread by the
-- older image.
--
-- Locks: ALTER TABLE takes ACCESS EXCLUSIVE on products, orders, customer_addresses and
-- shipping_areas until the migrator's single transaction commits. The orders and products CHECKs
-- scan tables of a few thousand rows at most, well under a second. lock_timeout bounds the wait
-- to acquire each lock, so the migration fails fast (and rolls back entirely) instead of queueing
-- every order read behind a long-running transaction. SET LOCAL lasts until that transaction ends.
SET LOCAL lock_timeout = '5s';--> statement-breakpoint
